Lan Mouse is a cross-platform mouse and keyboard sharing software
similar to universal-control on Apple devices. It allows for using
multiple PCs via a single set of mouse and keyboard (Software KVM switch).

Build & Runtime Dependencies:
  - rust / cargo (build dependency)
  - gtk4
  - libadwaita
  - libX11, libXtst (standard in Slackware x/ series)

Optional / Recommended Dependencies:
  - xdg-desktop-portal & libei (for Wayland / X11 input capture portal)
  - firewalld (optional firewall rules)

Slackware & Systemd-less Operation:
  Systemd is NOT required. `lan-mouse` can be started directly from your
  application menu, desktop environment autostart, or terminal:
    $ lan-mouse          # Starts GUI & daemon
    $ lan-mouse daemon   # Starts daemon only in CLI mode

X11 Support & Input Capture Note:
  X11 support (input emulation and capture flags) is explicitly compiled.
  - Input Emulation (Receiving input on an X11 machine): Fully supported out
    of the box using XTest.
  - Input Capture (Sending input from a host machine): On Linux, input capture
    relies on `libei` via `xdg-desktop-portal` (org.freedesktop.portal.InputCapture).
    If `xdg-desktop-portal` is not active, `lan-mouse` will display
    "input capture is disabled". Starting `xdg-desktop-portal` enables input
    capture capabilities.

Notes:
  To customize cargo build flags or features, CARGO_BUILD_FLAGS can be
  passed to the script:
    CARGO_BUILD_FLAGS="--no-default-features --features=..." ./lan-mouse.SlackBuild
